# IN THE MOOD – Glenn Miller
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
{ns}
{define: Am7 base-fret 1 frets x 0 2 0 1 0}
{define: CaddG base-fret 1 frets x 3 2 0 1 3} 
{define: D# base-fret 1 frets x x 5 3 4 3} 
{define: F3 base-fret 1 frets x x 5 4 3 3} 
{define: Gadd base-fret 1 frets 3 2 0 0 3 3} 
{t: IN THE MOOD – Glenn Miller  }
{st: (Razaf/Garland) }
{artist: Glenn Miller }
{composer: Razaf; Garland }


https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_CI-0E_jses  (Capo 1)

It's nearly impossible to properly translate horns into guitar but here goes.  This is all based on the Glenn Miller orchestra without singing (so no lyrics).  There is also a vocal version with the Andrews Sisters.

If transposing, play the root chord of the transposed (Gadd) and (CaddG) adding the 5th note as necessary.

TIP:  If you REALLY want to see how this could sound on an acoustic guitar
go here --->  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d6Nfp2Bgfa8

TIP:  For the intro, form (G) as an (F3) -- (F) on the 3rd fret - just another way to play (G) -- and hold your pinky aloft over the 2nd string.  Now pick the strings indicated beginning with the 4th string and walk down to the 1st.  Where you see (P2) that stands for "pinky on the 2nd string" so put your pinky down and pick the 2nd string once, lift off your pinky and pick it again, and then the 3rd string, then strum (G) as an (F3).  If you don't want to mess around with picking the individual notes then just strum (G), then (A) for two measures, and end with (A)(D7)(G).

TIP:  Where you see a a chord in brackets |G| or |CaddG| it means to emphasize the treble (g) with a sharp upstroke on the 1st string to emulate a trumpet.

TIP:  The (D#) is just like a (D) on the 3rd fret and cover the 4th string/5th fret with your pinky if you can. 

TIP:  There are timing cues if you're following along with the recording as to where you should be.

TIP:  The outro is the escalating trumpet.  Strum the chords repeatedly, moving to a crescendo with a final single stroke of (Gadd). 
 
     
